 ops ~ 50ms\n# p@v (output): 167M ops ~ 7ms\n# Total: ~175ms\n\n# Can we avoid p1@G by using the existing p1@v and some trick?\n# No, because p1@v gives us D=64 dims, but we need C=1280 dims for u1 norm.\n# Wait - u1 norm = ||p1 @ x|| in C-space. We don'\"'t have x in the current flow.\n\n# Actually, let me reconsider: \n# u0 = p0 @ x (C-space)\n# u1 = p1 @ x (C-space) \n# m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) (C-space)\n# output = (r * m) @ wv\"'^T (D-space)\n\n# If we have m in C-space, we can do m @ wv''^T for the output.\n# But we also need norms of u1 and m, which we can compute directly from m and u1.\n\n# Key question: is it better to:\n# (a) compute v upfront, use v-space attention, and Gram for norms\n# (b) compute in C-space, use C-space attention, and project at the end\n\n# Option b costs:\n#   q, k projections: 2 * B*L*C*H*D\n#   q@k''^T: B*H*L*L*D\n#   softmax x2\n#   p1@x: B*H*L*L*C\n#   p0@x: B*H*L*L*C\n#   m = (1-beta)*p0x + beta*p1x\n#   norms: cheap\n#   m @ wv''^T: B*L*C*H*D (one more projection)\n# Total big ops: 3*BLC*HD + 2*BHL*L*C + BHL*L*D\n\n# Option a costs:\n#   q, k, v projections: 3*B*L*C*H*D\n#   q@k''^T: BHL*L*D\n#   softmax x2\n#   p1@G: BHL*L*L\n#   pmix@G: BHL*L*L\n#   pmix@v: BHL*L*D\n# Total big ops: 3*BLC*HD + 2*BHL*L*L + 2*BHL*L*D\n\n# Compare: 2*BHL*L*C + BHL*L*D (option b) vs 2*BHL*L*L + 2*BHL*L*D (option a)\n# = 2*C + D vs 2*L + 2*D\n# For C=1280, L=256, D=64: 2*1280+64=2624 vs 2*256+128=640\n# So option a wins by 4x for this case.\n# For case with L=64, C=512: 2*512+64=1088 vs 2*64+128=256. Even bigger gap.\n\n# So Gram approach is solid. Let'\"'s look elsewhere.\n\n# What about computing G differently? \n# G = x @ x\"'^T, shape (B, L, L)\n# For B=2, L=256: 2*256*256*1280 = 168M ops. Relatively cheap.\n\n# What about softmax? 25ms for two softmaxes seems high.\n# Let'\"'s check: can we fuse p1_logits computation?\n\nlogits = torch.randn(B, H, L, L)\np1_logits = logits.clone()\na_p, a_m = 0.5 * 1.05 + 0.5, 0.5 * (1.0 - 1.05)  # dummy\n\ndef p1_softmax_current():\n    pl = logits.mul(a_p)\n    pl.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n    return pl.view(B*H*L, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\ndef p1_softmax_inplace():\n    pl = logits.clone()\n    pl.mul_(a_p)\n    pl.add_(logits.transpose(-1, -2), alpha=a_m)\n    return pl.view(B*H*L, L).softmax(dim=-1).view(B, H, L, L)\n\ntime_op('p1 softmax current', p1_softmax_current)\ntime_op('p1 softmax inplace', p1_softmax_inplace)\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"p1 softmax current: 29.08 ms\np1 softmax inplace: 26.93 ms\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 2, 256, 1280, 20, 64\ng = torch.Generator().manual_seed(17)\n\ndef time_op(name, fn, n=11):\n    fn()\n    t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n    for _ in range(n):\n        fn()\n    t1 = time.process_time_ns()\n    print(f'{name}: {(t1-t0)/n/1e6:.2f} ms')\n\n# Let's explore: can we reduce the 2 p@G matmuls to 1?\n# p_mix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1\n# We need both p1@G and pmix@G for norms.\n# ||p1@x||\"'^2 = sum((p1@G) * p1, dim=-1)\n# ||pmix@x||''^2 = sum((pmix@G) * pmix, dim=-1)\n\n# If we compute p1@G, can we get p0@G another way?\n# No, but we can derive ||m||''^2 from ||u0||''^2, ||u1||''^2, and <u0, u1>.\n# ||m||''^2 = ||(1-b)*u0 + b*u1||''^2 = (1-b)''^2*||u0||''^2 + b''^2*||u1||''^2 + 2*b*(1-b)*<u0,u1>\n# <u0,u1> = (p0@x) * (p1@x) summed over C = sum((p0@x) * (p1@x), dim=-1)\n#         = (p0 @ x @ x''^T @ p0''^T)... wait no\n# (u0 * u1).sum(-1) = row-wise dot product of u0 and u1\n# u0[i] = p0[i] @ x, u1[i] = p1[i] @ x  \n# <u0[i], u1[i]> = p0[i] @ x @ x''^T @ p1[i]''^T = p0[i] @ G @ p1[i]''^T\n# That'\"'s a full quadratic form. Not cheap.\n\n# Alternative: compute p1@G and derive both p0@G and pmix@G from it?\n# No - p0 and p1 are different matrices from different softmaxes.\n\n# What about: compute (p0 stacked with p1) @ G in one batched matmul?\n# That's the same amount of work as 2 separate matmuls since bmm has fixed batch dim.\n\n# Let me try the 2D-reshape trick for p@G\n# p shape: (B, H, L, L), G shape: (B, 1, L, L) broadcast -> (B, H, L, L)\n# Reshape to (B*H*L, L) @ (B*H, L, L)... no, G is per-batch.\n# Actually G is (B, L, L). p is (B, H, L, L).\n# p @ G.unsqueeze(1) is broadcasting G across H.\n# We can reshape p to (B*H, L, L) and G to (B, L, L), then... \n# Need to broadcast G along H dim, so expand and reshape.\n\np = torch.randn(B, H, L, L)\np = p.softmax(dim=-1)\nG = torch.randn(B, L, L)\nG_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)\n\ntime_op('p @ G_exp (4D broadcast)', lambda: p @ G_exp)\n\n# Alternative: reshape p to (B*H, L, L), expand G to (B*H, L, L)\np_2d = p.reshape(B*H, L, L)\nG_expand = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).reshape(B*H, L, L)\ntime_op('p@G (reshaped 3D bmm)', lambda: torch.bmm(p_2d, G_expand))\ntime_op('p@G (reshaped 3D @)', lambda: p_2d @ G_expand)\n\n# Another idea: use einsum 'bhij,bjk->bhik'\ntime_op('p@G (einsum)', lambda: torch.einsum('bhij,bjk->bhik', p, G))\n\n# What about using the existing logits structure?\n# No, G is x@x.T which is different from logits.\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
